<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raron</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Raron is a village in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-valais">Upper Valais</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> below the Bietschhorn. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was the seat of the powerful lords of Raron, and it later gave its name to one of the seven tenths, the self-governing districts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valais">Valais</a> republic. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rainer-maria-rilke">Rainer Maria Rilke</a> is buried beside its hilltop church.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raron.jpg" alt="Raron" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Berger · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raron3.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Raron is a municipality in the Westlich Raron district of the canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valais">Valais</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, and its district capital. It lies on the right side of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> valley at the entrance to the Bietsch valley, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visp">Visp</a>, and covers 30.3 square kilometres. The municipality includes the villages of Raron and Sankt German and the hamlets of Turtig and Rarnerchumma. Raron is first mentioned in the 12th century as Rarogni.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Finds near Raron show settlement from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> to the La Tène period, after which the site seems to have been abandoned in Roman times. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the hill was fortified. The Viztum of Raron was established in the 12th century and the Viztume tower house added, and the Raron, Asperlin and de Chevron-Villette families held the office of Vizedominat as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Sion. The Meier tower was built in the 13th century. The Viztume tower was partly destroyed in the Raron affair of 1417 and bought by the municipality in 1538, when it became town hall and jail.</p><p>A flood of the Bietschbach destroyed the church of St Romanus in 1494, except for its tower. Ulrich Ruffiner converted the Meier tower into a Gothic church in 1508 to 1514, dedicated to St Romanus. It was abandoned in the 1970s and replaced in 1974 by a modern rock church at the foot of the hill. The large stone houses of Raron record the prosperity of the 17th and 18th centuries, which owed much to traffic over the St German pass to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visp">Visp</a>. The draining of the swamps from 1865 to 1885 and the Lonza canal of 1920 opened the valley to agriculture.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Raron had a population of 1,949 in December 2020, and in 2000 most people spoke German as their first language. A military airfield built in the 1940s was abandoned in 1994, and the south portal of the Lötschberg Base Tunnel opened east of the village in 2007. The prehistoric site of Heidnischbühl, the Church of St Roman and the Viztume tower house are Swiss heritage sites of national significance. Raron is known for periodic Passion plays, and the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rainer-maria-rilke">Rainer Maria Rilke</a> is buried in the cemetery of the old church.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Raron” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The core county passed to the barons of <b>Raron</b>, who sold it to the Imperial Abbey of Saint Gall in 1468.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-toggenburg">County of Toggenburg</a></li><li><blockquote>After decades of struggle, the Zenden forced Bishop Hildebrand Jost to abandon most of his temporal rights in 1628 and finally in 1634, and the Valais became a republic of seven tenths: Goms, Brig, Visp, <b>Raron</b>, Leuk, Sierre and Sion.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-the-seven-tenths">Republic of the Seven Tenths</a></li></ul></section>
